# Ontology

This branch began as **Lingua**, and Lingua began as one question: how can two
people who have never met use the same word and be sure they mean the same
thing. It turned out to be the smaller half of a larger question. Before you
can share a meaning you have to be able to hold one, and holding one is the
whole problem — what a thing is, what has been said about it, who said it, and
what any of that is worth to the person reading it later.

So Ontology stopped being a vocabulary and became the floor. It is the small
set of shapes everything above is made from, and the reason Lince does not grow
a new table every time it grows a feature. Four shapes carry nearly all of it:

> **Records model things. Assertions model what is said about them. Concepts
> name those assertions. Lingua lets different Organs share or translate their
> meanings.**

Everything else is a projection of those four, a constraint on them, or a
convenience over them. Protein is a way of reading them. Sync is a way of
moving them between Organs. A Transfer is a promise about them; a Karma rule is
arithmetic over them. When something new is proposed here the first question is
which of the four it already is — and a genuinely fifth shape has not been
needed yet.

The parts below take one shape each, with the room to explain themselves.
